ectional_derivative_7e0ae474_005","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"How does the directional derivative relate to the total derivative?","options":["$\\mathrm{D}_{\\boldsymbol{v}}\\boldsymbol{f}(\\boldsymbol{x}_0) = \\mathrm{D}\\boldsymbol{f}_{\\boldsymbol{x}_0}(\\boldsymbol{v})$","$\\mathrm{D}_{\\boldsymbol{v}}\\boldsymbol{f}(\\boldsymbol{x}_0) = \\boldsymbol{f}(\\boldsymbol{v})$","$\\mathrm{D}_{\\boldsymbol{v}}\\boldsymbol{f}(\\boldsymbol{x}_0) = \\boldsymbol{x}_0+\\boldsymbol{v}$","$\\mathrm{D}_{\\boldsymbol{v}}\\boldsymbol{f}(\\boldsymbol{x}_0) = 0$ for every $\\boldsymbol{v}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d5c18b52365ace25acf6563b","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_directional_derivative_7e0ae474_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For $\\boldsymbol{f}(x,y) = (x^2,y^2)$, $\\boldsymbol{x}_0 = (1,2)$, and $\\boldsymbol{v} = (1,0)$, what is the directional derivative?","options":["$(1,0)$","$(0,4)$","$(2,4)$","$(2,0)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8adf844ec5fcf1d37c01ec11","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_directional_derivative_7e0ae474_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Which unit vector points in the same direction as $(3,4)$?","options":["$(\\frac{3}{5},\\frac{4}{5})$","$(3,4)$","$(\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5})$","$(\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{4}{3})$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6234613db14cc7be3587bb1a","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_directional_derivative_7e0ae474_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type"